00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.860
Frederick Douglass had heard the stories

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:56.410
every slave had whispered accounts

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.810
of fugitives running from the slave catchers

00:02:58.810 --> 00:02:59.750
for nights on end.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:04.140
Coming within sight of freedom, only to hear the hounds closing in.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:10.230
Fugitives starving, freezing to death, being torn apart by wild animals.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:13.700
Everyone knew what would happen if you were caught.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:20.570
Flogging, branding, elaborate tortures designed to strike terror into any other would-be runaways.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.300
Some lost hope and returned to their masters.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.960
Others killed themselves, rather than go back to slavery.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.220
But some made it.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:42.880
In the fall of 1838, Frederick Douglass leapt aboard a train in Baltimore, slipped through

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:47.920
the gauntlet of slave catchers prowling the border between slave and free states, and finally

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.060
boarded a ferry bound for New York City.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:58.490
Only 24 hours removed from slavery, Douglass found himself wandering among the dazzling

00:03:58.490 --> 00:03:59.490
wonders of Broadway.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:08.080
A free state around me and the free earth under my feet.

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:12.030
What a moment this was for me.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.270
A whole year was pressed into a single day.

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:20.650
A new world burst upon my agitated vision.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:26.220
Douglas had planned his escape with the help of Anna Murray,

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:28.860
a free black woman he had met in Baltimore.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.540
Anna had sewn his sailor disguise and sold her feather bed to give him the money he needed.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:40.600
As soon as she joined him in New York, they were married in a safe house by a fellow runaway.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.180
There was no time to rest. New York was crawling with bounty hunters.

00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:53.380
With five dollars to their names, Frederick and Anna headed further north.

00:04:56.420 --> 00:04:58.900
Their destination was New Bedford, Massachusetts.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.700
a wailing town known as an abolitionist stronghold

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:06.620
with a large population of free blacks and runaway slaves.

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.470
Frederick and Anna clawed their way out of desperate poverty

00:05:12.470 --> 00:05:14.830
and built a life for their growing family.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:21.060
For three years, Douglas shoveled coal, cut wood, and loaded ships,

00:05:21.580 --> 00:05:23.340
often working two shifts a day.

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:27.590
He did make time to attend anti-slavery gatherings

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:29.980
and was an avid reader of the most...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.940
prominent abolitionist newspaper of the day, William Lloyd Garrison's The Liberator.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:44.120
I not only liked, I loved this paper and its editor. He seemed a match for all the opponents

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:54.630
of emancipation. Its words were few and full of holy fire. When Douglas heard that Garrison

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:59.680
was going to address an anti-slavery convention in Nantucket, he took a rare few days off.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.550
and boarded a ferry for the island.

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.500
As Douglas listened to the proceedings,

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:09.060
a friend from New Bedford unexpectedly called on him to speak.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:13.390
As I made my way to the stage, Douglas recalled,

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.090
I was shaking in every limb.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.480
My mother was named Harriet Bailey.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:29.500
I can't recollect of ever seeing her by the light of day.

00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:35.240
We were separated when I was but an infant.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.180
My father was a white man.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:47.740
It was whispered that my master was my father, but of this, I know nothing.

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:53.290
What I do know is that my master was a very cruel man.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:29.660
Just a bit of slavery.

00:07:58.830 --> 00:07:59.250
Mr. Douglas.

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:12.360
i have never seen an audience so captivated i myself was carried away as you saw

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:19.180
i was so nervous i could barely stand wonderful if you don't mind my asking

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:29.610
how did you first realize that you were a slave slowly

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:34.220
I was raised by my grandmother.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.080
We lived far from the slave quarters,

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:41.100
and so I didn't know what went on there.

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:47.130
And one day when I was six years old,

00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:53.180
my grandmother was told to walk me to the workhouse

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.110
near the master's place.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:59.960
She sent me over to play with the other children, and...

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:14.250
abandoned me dear dear lord she loved me but she had no choice what a horrible horrible crime

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:25.270
an innocent child why were you raised by your grandmother what what about your mother my mother

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:29.740
only saw her a few times

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:37.760
At night, she was taken away after I was born to work as a field hand 12 miles away.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:48.080
She walked all the way back a handful of times, but even so, they never let me know her enough to really love her.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:55.310
I'd left a hole. I can't express it.

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:59.970
My mother left me too. I was five.

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:05.810
She worked in another town for a few years. I missed her terribly

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:18.730
But but that that that it was nothing compared with no sir. It's never nothing dear. Mr. Douglas join me

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:28.180
I urge you you have a gift sir. You really do you flatter me no

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:30.700
No, I don't.

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:37.960
Mr. Gasson, you must understand how unexpected this is.

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:39.740
I never imagined...

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:46.080
You have an opportunity to strike a bow for the slaves.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.960
Continue to suffer as we speak.

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:56.260
The Lord gave you these gifts so that you could spend your life working in the shipyards.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:10:59.880
I see now.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.780
why you are such an effective advocate.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:03.620
You'll do it then.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.030
I think I will.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:17.140
Put on the whole armor of God.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.060
Douglas's new life was fraught with danger.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:23.900
He was a fugitive.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:27.790
By speaking out in public, he risked capture by bounty hunters.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:33.510
But he understood that his testimony could help transform the struggle.

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.560
Garrison and his followers had failed to convert the slaveholders.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.780
Now, they hope to deprive them of their financial and political support in the North.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.800
Douglas and his fellow agents were set out to recruit the foot soldiers for this campaign,

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:55.420
the men and women who would organize boycotts, raise money, and petition Congress.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:59.100
You must not give or take any quarter.

00:11:59.340 --> 00:11:59.720
You!

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:03.620
must cleave slavery to the ground.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:11.670
Thank you.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:17.210
Now, I would like to introduce to you

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.590
a graduate from the peculiar institution.

00:12:23.990 --> 00:12:27.830
He can bear witness to the true nature of that institution.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:29.830
His diploma is written.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.570
on his back, Mr. Frederick Douglass.

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.350
Douglass is something really, really special.

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:49.210
And Garrison can sense that this is a way

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:53.620
to really make the abolitionist cause incarnate.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:58.020
Many of the audience members had never, ever seen a slave,

00:12:58.420 --> 00:12:59.980
let alone been to the slave.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:09.620
South. So to have a person like Douglas gave the anti-slavery cause teeth. It gave

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:17.470
it authenticity. It gave it a new voice. Over the course of a few months, Douglas

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:22.760
traveled three and a half thousand miles. The campaign was bearing fruit.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.120
Northerners were flooding Congress with anti-slavery petitions.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.120
passed a gag rule forbidding their consideration. It added to northern

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.760
suspicions that the government was operating for the benefit of slave

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:43.220
holders. Abolitionists had driven another wedge into the fractures of the Union,

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:53.280
but for Douglas that wasn't enough. He wanted to affect change directly. When

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:57.350
he heard that a fellow runaway had been captured, he saw a chance to enlist

00:13:57.350 --> 00:13:58.530
Garrison and his followers.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:07.020
Dear friend Garrison, slavery, our enemy, has landed in our very midst.

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:14.930
George Latimer has been hunted down like a wild beast and imprisoned in Leverett Street Jail.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:25.560
George Latimer had arrived in Boston in the fall of 1842, only to be thrown in jail at the request of a Virginia planter.

00:14:27.660 --> 00:14:29.960
Boston has become the hunting ground.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:32.120
of merciless man-stealers.

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:36.710
We need not point to the sugar fields of Louisiana

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:39.370
or to the rice swamps of Alabama

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:42.270
for the bloody deeds of this soul-crushing system,

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.960
but to the city of pilgrims.

00:14:48.090 --> 00:14:50.930
Garrison and the other abolitionists took up the call.

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:55.090
Soon, Boston was in an uproar.

00:14:56.980 --> 00:14:58.200
Fearing an assault on the jail,

00:14:58.850 --> 00:14:59.970
the sheriff released Latham.

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.340
and his master had no choice but to set him free.

00:15:05.030 --> 00:15:07.660
For abolitionists, it was a stunning victory,

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.520
but they weren't done.

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:14.490
In a show of strength, they collected 65,000 signatures,

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:16.490
bound the documents together,

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.620
and rolled them like barrels into the state house.

00:15:21.950 --> 00:15:26.520
March of 1843, Massachusetts complied with their demands

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.660
by passing the Personal Liberty Act.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.690
State officials could never again take part in the recapture of a fugitive slave.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:39.700
Within a few years, almost every other northern state had followed suit.

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:46.310
Personal liberty laws set alarm bells ringing throughout the South.

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:50.630
Southerners have a right to be outraged.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:57.300
Here we have proof positive that there is an utter disregard for the compromises that

00:15:57.300 --> 00:15:59.760
have been made found this American republic.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.920
to try to keep this thing running.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:09.240
You see the South rising to say,

00:16:09.690 --> 00:16:12.770
this is yet another assault on our way of life.

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:16.140
This cannot be.

00:16:18.150 --> 00:16:22.120
With every headline, every petition, every controversy,

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.600
the abolitionists were dragging the fight over slavery

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:27.650
to the center of national life.

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:29.970
That...

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.520
was starting to tear the country apart.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:51.670
By the winter of 1845, Frederick Douglass

00:16:51.670 --> 00:16:54.670
had been touring the anti-slavery circuit for four years.

00:16:55.660 --> 00:16:57.220
As he contemplated his future,

00:16:57.950 --> 00:16:59.790
Douglass could imagine himself traveling

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.130
forever in the shadow of his white mentors, repeating his story to small gatherings of

00:17:06.130 --> 00:17:07.590
the curious and the converted.

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:15.090
Tell your story, Frederick, would whisper my friend William Lloyd Garrison as we stepped

00:17:15.090 --> 00:17:15.970
upon the platform.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:23.250
I cannot always obey, for it did not satisfy me to simply narrate wrongs.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:25.770
I felt like denouncing them.

00:17:27.140 --> 00:17:29.140
Besides, I was growing.

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:42.240
room that winter douglas decided to reach out to a vast new audience throwing caution to the winds

00:17:42.240 --> 00:17:56.830
he published the story of his life tuckahoe in talbert county maryland i have no accurate

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:59.970
knowledge of my age by far the law

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.740
part of slaves know as little of their age as horses know of theirs, and it is

00:18:06.740 --> 00:18:14.120
the wish of most masters to keep their slaves thus ignorant. It's an expose

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:21.760
about, of course, the horrible treatment of slaves. It's also an example of how

00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:27.460
the institution of slavery not only degrades slaves, but it degrades the

00:18:27.460 --> 00:18:27.840
master.

00:18:32.840 --> 00:18:38.380
Douglas listed dates, places, and most dangerous of all, his owner's name.

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:43.970
One white friend read the document and advised Douglas to burn it.

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:48.830
Should Douglas' owner ever want to reclaim his property, he warned,

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:51.840
there was nothing anyone could do to protect him.

00:18:53.660 --> 00:18:55.500
Douglas would have none of it.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:59.460
It was truly a cultural event.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.340
It was an immediate and an immense bestseller.

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:10.680
That narrative was published in 1845.

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:16.160
By 1846 to 1847, Douglass was a household name

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:17.520
in the United States.

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:20.460
Among those who read the book

00:19:20.640 --> 00:19:23.210
was Douglass's owner, Thomas Auld.

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:27.520
He called Douglass a liar and vowed to track him down

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:29.980
and send him to the cotton fields of the deep south.

00:19:30.030 --> 00:19:34.940
Douglas fled the country for Great Britain.

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:40.100
There, he could carry on Garrison's campaign to isolate the South

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:43.330
by urging Britons to cut ties with American slaveholders.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:47.720
The experience would change his life.

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:54.950
Douglas's time in Britain was the first time in his life

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:57.550
where he experienced a dearth of racism.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.620
first time where he could walk down the street and not have someone spit at him,

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:08.120
not have someone scowl and call him a nigger. That was the first time he could

00:20:08.120 --> 00:20:11.660
walk into any public establishment and not have someone kick him out.

00:20:14.190 --> 00:20:18.230
As Douglass's narrative became a bestseller, he was treated by his British

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:26.180
hosts not just as an equal, but as a celebrity. Most important of all, for the

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:29.640
first time in his life, Douglass was free.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:36.440
His British sympathizers sent Thomas Ald a note saying,

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.480
we have your slave, let's negotiate.

00:20:39.180 --> 00:20:42.200
He's probably worth $1,500, $2,000, we'll give you $800.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:44.850
Try to recover him.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:47.130
The Alds agreed to it.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:52.250
So when Douglass returns to the United States,

00:20:52.330 --> 00:20:53.570
he's legally free.

00:20:55.380 --> 00:20:59.980
Douglass loved England and Ireland and Scotland so much that he...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.300
he almost stayed there.

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:07.210
The only reason that he decided to return to the United States

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:11.160
is because he felt a sense of responsibility and obligation

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:12.280
to his fellow blacks.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:16.820
Douglas had fled the United States alone,

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.290
a fugitive running for his life.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:25.820
When he left London to return home in the spring of 1847,

00:21:26.950 --> 00:21:29.730
1,400 people came to see him off.

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:40.860
Shortly after landing in Boston, Douglas joined Garrison for a speaking tour of Ohio.

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:46.240
I seem to have undergone a transformation, Douglas told his old mentor.

00:21:47.070 --> 00:21:48.920
I live a new life.

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:55.940
At each stop, Garrison was reminded that his protege was coming to eclipse him.

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.970
At one of their first appearances, Garrison couldn't...

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.040
even finish his speech as the audience drowned him out with chants of,

00:22:04.340 --> 00:22:12.790
Douglas, Douglas. And Douglas had a secret. His friends in Britain had raised money so that he

00:22:12.790 --> 00:22:18.460
could launch his own anti-slavery newspaper. He quickly realized that Garrison and his allies

00:22:18.460 --> 00:22:23.620
were vehemently opposed to the notion, so Douglas kept his old mentor in the dark.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:35.760
A few weeks into the tour, the relentless pace and grueling conditions began to tell on Garrison.

00:22:36.880 --> 00:22:43.480
In Cleveland, he spoke to three large rallies in a single day, standing for hours in a cold rain.

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:47.320
That evening, Garrison collapsed.

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:53.150
For three days, he was lost in a terrifying haze of fever and delirium.

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:59.970
My dear wife, I'm going to try to write you a few lines.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:12.630
with my own hand I have lost 20 pounds and am quite thin and weak. Douglas

00:23:12.630 --> 00:23:17.060
reluctantly left Garrison in Cleveland to finish the speaking tour on his own.

00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:26.980
His secret leaked out shortly after he left Garrison on his sick bed. Garrison

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:29.750
has to learn that his this

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:38.400
man that he mentored and his close friend is leaving him, abandoning him, and Douglas doesn't

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:46.270
even tell him firsthand. He saw himself as responsible as anyone for Douglas' success,

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:51.520
for Douglas' fame, for Douglas' popularity. He never forgives Douglas.

00:23:53.970 --> 00:23:59.870
My dear wife, is it not strange that Douglas has not written a single line to me since

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:07.970
left me on a bed of illness. In regard to his project for establishing a paper, he never

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:16.920
spoke a word to me on the subject, nor asked my advice in any particular whatsoever. Such

00:24:16.920 --> 00:24:18.740
conduct grieves me to the heart.

00:24:31.340 --> 00:24:35.320
In the fall of 1847, a few weeks after leaving garrison,

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:37.900
Douglas moved to Rochester, New York.

00:24:38.890 --> 00:24:40.430
Just over the border from Canada,

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:43.830
Rochester was the last stop on the Underground Railroad,

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:48.610
the network of safe houses used by slaves fleeing north to safety.

00:24:51.900 --> 00:24:56.370
The city's vibrant abolitionist community welcomed Douglas with open arms.

00:24:58.760 --> 00:24:59.960
As soon as he arrived...

00:25:00.540 --> 00:25:02.700
Douglass set about producing his own paper,

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:05.770
naming it after the most potent symbol

00:25:05.770 --> 00:25:07.150
of the Underground Railroad.

00:25:08.240 --> 00:25:11.160
Most fugitives, if they knew anything

00:25:11.160 --> 00:25:16.300
about the route to freedom, it was follow the North Star.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:21.550
Douglass' location allows him to be very involved

00:25:21.550 --> 00:25:22.770
in the Underground Railroad.

00:25:23.820 --> 00:25:26.000
He wrote that in the mornings he would come

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:29.980
and find 10 or 11 fugitive slaves sitting on the...

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:35.100
the doorstep, waiting for him to arrive in his office so that he could help shuttle them

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:35.740
to Canada.

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:41.300
For the first few months, Douglas' newspaper hemorrhaged money.

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:47.080
He had few subscribers and no experience as either a printer or a businessman.

00:25:48.210 --> 00:25:53.280
He mortgaged his house to pay his debts and looked about to give up when a friend arrived

00:25:53.280 --> 00:25:54.280
from Britain to help.

00:25:56.150 --> 00:25:59.980
Julia Griffith's editorial skills and business savvy kept the North

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:07.130
star afloat. But her appearance in Rochester sparked rumors, and when she moved into Douglass's

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:16.290
home, those rumors blossomed into a full-blown scandal. Douglass brushed the talk aside. He

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:22.890
was invigorated in his new surroundings. Far from Garrison's Boston headquarters, Douglass

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:28.420
was free to explore the political, and even militant, anti-slavery strategies that were

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:29.460
circulating in Rochester.

00:26:31.630 --> 00:26:36.420
As he did so, he made the acquaintance of a man whose name he had heard in whispers,

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:40.760
failed tanner and fervent abolitionist John Brown.

00:26:41.240 --> 00:26:43.000
May I get you anything else, Mr. Douglas?

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:45.180
Thank you. No, Mrs. Brown.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:48.180
A meal like this is a rare pleasure these days.

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:49.700
A pleasure is ours, Mr. Douglas.

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:52.700
I'd hoped to meet you long before this.

00:26:53.930 --> 00:26:54.690
I'm flattered, sir.

00:26:56.380 --> 00:26:58.960
Your speeches have been an inspiration to us.

00:27:00.670 --> 00:27:06.120
I do wonder, though, if the speeches will ever be enough.

00:27:07.600 --> 00:27:08.300
What do you mean, sir?

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:14.370
I've spent this for years.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.000
Freedom is a long road, Mr. Brown.

00:27:22.720 --> 00:27:23.780
I don't know any shortcuts.

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:29.070
Do you, Douglas?

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:31.230
I do.

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:35.330
Sir.

00:27:58.820 --> 00:27:59.980
God has placed these mount-

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:04.440
You know God's thinking?

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.160
I know these mountains.

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:12.480
From here we can strike a blow against the slave masters.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:17.010
The mountains are full of natural forts.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.600
One good man could hold off a hundred soldiers,

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:26.160
take hand-picked men,

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:29.980
and post them in squads of five on a line here.

00:28:31.120 --> 00:28:37.630
They come down off the mountains, raid the plantations, bring off the slaves, offer them a chance to fight.

00:28:39.780 --> 00:28:41.040
Sir, you have no idea.

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:44.020
The entire state of Virginia will rise up against you.

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:45.960
They will fight you and claw.

00:28:46.290 --> 00:28:47.750
Colored people must fight back.

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:52.510
They will never respect themselves otherwise, nor will they be respected.

00:28:53.600 --> 00:28:54.580
I read your book, sir.

00:28:55.520 --> 00:28:58.420
You said yourself you became a man when you fought Mr. Covey.

00:28:59.500 --> 00:28:59.900
I did.

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:01.880
But I was young, and this is very different.

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:05.380
We must follow in our Savior's footsteps.

00:29:06.180 --> 00:29:07.600
We must convert the sinner.

00:29:08.350 --> 00:29:10.130
This is the sin right here.

00:29:11.420 --> 00:29:14.160
We sit here, all of us, debating this point of law.

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:16.930
Whether the Constitution says this or that.

00:29:18.980 --> 00:29:21.380
In the meantime, day after day and year after year,

00:29:21.460 --> 00:29:23.220
the slaveholders are free to do their worst.

00:29:26.950 --> 00:29:29.630
If we stoop to bloodshed, we are no better than they are.

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.660
and preach for all maternity and nothing will ever change.

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:39.070
Mr. Douglas, how many slaveholders have you converted?

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:41.850
How many slaves have you freed?

00:29:47.490 --> 00:29:50.850
John Brown had a very beguiling personality.

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:53.690
He was a stunning man.

00:29:54.680 --> 00:29:59.000
His sense of moral commitment was vivid and overwhelming.

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:03.800
He was the real thing.

00:30:04.960 --> 00:30:07.160
And to a Frederick Douglass, he was also the real thing

00:30:07.160 --> 00:30:09.900
in terms of actually believing about as deeply

00:30:09.900 --> 00:30:13.930
as anybody Douglass had ever met in racial equality.

00:30:16.190 --> 00:30:17.270
Soon after their meeting,

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:20.100
Douglass described Brown in the North Star

00:30:20.100 --> 00:30:22.160
as someone who, though a white gentleman,

00:30:22.840 --> 00:30:24.940
is as deeply interested in our cause

00:30:24.940 --> 00:30:29.450
as though his own soul had been pierced with the iron of slavery.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:35.320
Slaveholders have forfeited even the right to live.

00:30:36.400 --> 00:30:39.520
And if the slaves should put every one of them to the sword tomorrow,

00:30:40.850 --> 00:30:43.510
who would say that they deserve anything less than death?

00:30:52.180 --> 00:30:54.140
As Douglas was meeting with John Brown,

00:30:54.860 --> 00:30:57.020
a chain of events was being set in motion

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:59.400
that would transform the future of slavery.

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:08.140
of America. In the spring of 1846, the United States went to war with Mexico, hoping to

00:31:08.140 --> 00:31:15.810
gain vast territories in the southwest. Abolitionists bitterly opposed the war as an attempt to

00:31:15.810 --> 00:31:22.180
expand slave territory, but they were swept away by a national tide of patriotic enthusiasm.

00:31:22.400 --> 00:31:29.660
The Mexican War ultimately increases the size of the United States by virtually

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:36.430
100% it almost doubles the size and the big question is what are we gonna do

00:31:36.430 --> 00:31:41.330
with all this land acquired from Mexico slave owners wanted all to be slave

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:46.780
territory anti-slave northerners all wanted it to be free territory the

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:52.880
Mexican war unshucked slavery it just took it out of its shell all those

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:59.980
efforts to contain this issue couldn't work anymore well southerners saw the

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.660
expansion of slave territory as a guarantee that the institution would continue to thrive

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:13.870
northerners viewed those plans as a conspiracy to build a true slave empire northerners become

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:20.780
convinced that southerners are hell-bent on moving slavery to every part of the country and now you

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:26.240
have this political fight going on over what's going to happen with that land and that becomes

00:32:26.240 --> 00:32:28.900
very, very divisive very quickly.

00:32:34.190 --> 00:32:39.320
Through 1847 and 1848, the question of the new territories festered.

00:32:40.160 --> 00:32:43.210
In the answer to that question lay the country's destiny.

00:32:43.390 --> 00:32:59.340
In the summer of 1849...

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:05.120
The fight over slavery was suddenly eclipsed as cholera swept through cities across the country.

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:12.580
Incessant rains flooded the streets with sewage that spread the epidemic with terrifying speed.

00:33:15.710 --> 00:33:22.350
Outside Cincinnati, Harriet Beecher Stowe followed reports of the disease as it swept through the slums downtown.

00:33:24.470 --> 00:33:29.730
Stowe had hoped to become a writer, but now she was too busy raising six young children.

00:33:30.220 --> 00:33:36.580
on very little money. It was all she could do to send accounts of home life to her husband,

00:33:37.130 --> 00:33:45.050
who was working out of town for the summer. Yesterday, I went downtown and found all gloomy

00:33:45.050 --> 00:33:50.680
and discouraged. A universal panic seems to be drawing nearer than ever before.

00:33:52.610 --> 00:33:58.360
Hurst drivers have scarce been allowed to unharness their horses. Furniture carts and

00:33:58.360 --> 00:33:59.980
common vehicles are being imported.

00:34:00.000 --> 00:34:01.520
for the removal of the dead.

00:34:05.630 --> 00:34:08.690
Stowe had every reason to hope that her family would be spared.

00:34:09.510 --> 00:34:12.800
They lived far from the tenements, where the poor were being decimated.

00:34:13.700 --> 00:34:17.300
She kept the children close to home and maintained a cheerful disposition.

00:34:19.600 --> 00:34:24.880
But at the beginning of July, Charlie, Stowe's beloved little boy, was taken ill.

00:34:26.720 --> 00:34:29.650
She called him her summer child, Harriet.

00:34:30.760 --> 00:34:35.260
wrote such glowing praise of Charlie

00:34:35.260 --> 00:34:37.960
that her husband was actually disturbed by it.

00:34:39.830 --> 00:34:43.770
She said she never had such an easy time with a baby.

00:34:44.050 --> 00:34:47.390
And indeed, it was the first baby she was able to nurse herself.

00:34:51.720 --> 00:34:56.680
I could not help nor soothe nor do one thing

00:34:56.680 --> 00:34:58.940
to mitigate his cruel suffering.

00:35:01.760 --> 00:35:04.580
Do nothing but pray that he might die soon.

00:35:11.910 --> 00:35:13.230
It was a terrible disease.

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:16.500
You lost all the fluids in your body.

00:35:16.640 --> 00:35:17.980
You were wracked by convulsions.

00:35:18.600 --> 00:35:20.340
And there was nothing that anybody could do.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:44.930
at last it is over and our dear little one is gone from us my charlie my baby so loving and sweet

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:55.210
so full of life and hope and strength now lies shrouded pale and cold in the room below

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:11.740
In the Calvinist scheme of things, if God sent you suffering,

00:36:12.000 --> 00:36:15.670
it was because he loved you and he wanted to teach you something.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:24.780
Stone later wrote that there were circumstances of such great bitterness

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:27.040
about the manner of his death

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:29.980
that she didn't think she could ever be reconciled for it.

00:36:30.640 --> 00:36:33.980
unless his death allows her to do some great good to others.

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:43.100
For Harriet Beecher Stowe, the wound of Charlie's death would never heal.

00:36:44.980 --> 00:36:49.150
Rather, she came to understand the pain of mothers she would never know.

00:36:52.060 --> 00:36:59.970
It was at his dying bed and at his grave that I learned what a poor slave mother may feel when her child is torn away.

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:09.430
from her. She was really fueled by the grief for her baby, but I suspect to

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:18.410
perhaps anger at a Calvinist God. The writing of Uncle Tom's Cabin would be

00:37:18.410 --> 00:37:22.570
Harriet's way of making her baby's short life mean something.

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:42.460
In the spring of 1850, the question of the territories seized in the Mexican War

00:37:42.460 --> 00:37:46.220
finally exploded in the gravest crisis in the nation's history.

00:37:48.330 --> 00:37:51.650
California wanted to be admitted to the Union as a free state.

00:37:52.890 --> 00:37:58.000
If that happened, the balance of power in Washington would shift against the slave states.

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.460
Southerners perceived a mortal threat and talked openly of secession.

00:38:04.570 --> 00:38:07.410
Representatives brandished revolvers in the halls of Congress,

00:38:08.050 --> 00:38:11.170
and the nation contemplated the imminent collapse of the Union.

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:16.320
For Garrison and many other abolitionists,

00:38:16.860 --> 00:38:20.460
it seemed that the slave power might finally suffer a fatal blow.

00:38:24.400 --> 00:38:28.020
But in the fall of 1850, the country stepped back from the brink.

00:38:28.620 --> 00:38:29.960
When Congress adopted...

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:37.340
became known as the great compromise in return for allowing california to join the union as a free

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:43.700
state southerners were granted the prospect of someday forming slave states in utah and new

00:38:43.700 --> 00:38:50.740
mexico but for northerners the most galling provision in the compromise was the fugitive

00:38:50.740 --> 00:38:59.860
slave law law stipulated that any citizen north or south could be rounded up and forced to catch

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.240
a suspected runaway.

00:39:03.930 --> 00:39:06.970
Fugitive Slave Law of 1850 virtually legitimates

00:39:06.970 --> 00:39:08.590
the kidnapping of free blacks.

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:14.540
It means that a southerner can hunt down any black

00:39:14.540 --> 00:39:17.440
in free soil and say, you're my slave.

00:39:18.370 --> 00:39:21.470
And most significantly in one sense,

00:39:21.570 --> 00:39:25.050
any white can be deputized at any moment, day or night,

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:29.660
and is required to help round up a suspected fugitive slave.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:36.060
When the compromise was finally sealed in late September, abolitionists were horrified.

00:39:37.710 --> 00:39:42.660
Twenty years of struggle had yielded not emancipation, but a million more slaves

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:48.020
and a political agreement to preserve the institution in the United States forever.

00:39:50.810 --> 00:39:59.540
The petitions, the campaigns, the rallies, the marches, the meetings and resolutions and fundraisers, the mobs.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:05.710
beatings. All of the sacrifices had been suddenly dealt away by a handful of men

00:40:05.710 --> 00:40:12.660
in Washington. As a final insult, abolitionists were cast as villains who

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:18.280
had almost torn the nation apart. The New York Globe urged that no public

00:40:18.280 --> 00:40:23.620
building, not even the streets, must be desecrated by such a gathering of

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:29.000
traitors. It became dangerous once again to speak out against slavery.

00:40:31.310 --> 00:40:37.270
You just had a huge blow to your belief that somehow you're going to end slavery in this country

00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:42.450
by appealing to the Christian soul of the country.

00:40:43.730 --> 00:40:48.500
What the country just said, the heart of the government, is that slavery is forever.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:56.540
This radicalizes the anti-slavery movement arguably more than anything that had ever happened before.

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:03.100
The crisis convinced Garrison that he needed to redouble his efforts.

00:41:04.940 --> 00:41:10.580
From Boston, he directed an energetic campaign of meetings, petitions, and publicity.

00:41:11.930 --> 00:41:17.000
But for many abolitionists, Garrison's old tactics seemed hopelessly inadequate.

00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:25.580
Frederick Douglass, for one, had begun to question his own faith in a peaceful end to

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:28.420
slavery and in American democracy itself.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:31.720
Liberty is an unholy license.

00:41:32.500 --> 00:41:35.160
Your national greatness swelling vanity.

00:41:36.010 --> 00:41:39.250
Your shouts of liberty and equality, hollow mockery.

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:44.470
Your prayers and hymns are fraud, deception, impiety and hypocrisy.

00:41:45.100 --> 00:41:49.400
A thin veil to cover crimes which would disgrace a nation of savages.

00:41:50.530 --> 00:41:55.960
There's not a nation on earth guilty of practices more shocking and bloody

00:41:55.960 --> 00:41:59.970
than are the people of the United States at this very hour.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:02.960
It is not light that is needed, but fire.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:05.700
It is not the gentle shower, but thunder.

00:42:06.550 --> 00:42:08.890
We need the storm, the whirlwind, and the earthquake.

00:42:09.210 --> 00:42:12.310
Douglas needs for slavery to be eradicated,

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:15.770
and he needs it to happen as quickly as possible.

00:42:16.460 --> 00:42:21.180
In many ways, he reflects this for the rest of black America,

00:42:21.180 --> 00:42:26.020
that while Garrisonian tactics were just earnest,

00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:29.960
they simply were not options that he...

00:42:30.030 --> 00:42:34.070
thought would eventually lead to the end of slavery.

00:42:34.250 --> 00:42:36.870
On the one level, you hate to reduce it to race.

00:42:38.190 --> 00:42:39.970
On the other, it's a reality.

00:42:42.100 --> 00:42:44.600
No matter how many stories Garrison hears,

00:42:44.970 --> 00:42:47.150
no matter how many coffles he sees,

00:42:47.390 --> 00:42:51.350
no matter how many mothers he can imagine and conjure up,

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.680
Douglas has had that experience.

00:42:55.770 --> 00:42:59.090
Garrison is a white man in a white man's America.

00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:09.160
When Douglass questioned Garrison's doctrines at a meeting of the Anti-Slavery Society,

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:11.060
he was branded an enemy.

00:43:12.270 --> 00:43:15.510
A volley of insults and allegations appeared in the Liberator.

00:43:17.750 --> 00:43:21.530
Douglass struck back, accusing Garrison of racism.

00:43:23.320 --> 00:43:29.500
Final blow came when Garrison published ugly insinuations about an affair between Douglass

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:31.080
and Julia Griffiths.

00:43:33.100 --> 00:43:36.080
Garrison has hit Douglas below the belt.

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:38.980
It's like a family quarrel,

00:43:39.200 --> 00:43:42.180
where they're airing each other's dirty laundry,

00:43:42.400 --> 00:43:43.820
and it's not a pretty sight.

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:49.510
Mr. Garrison has seemed fit to invade my household,

00:43:50.850 --> 00:43:53.390
and has sought to blast me in the name of my family.

00:43:57.870 --> 00:43:59.830
Griffiths would eventually bow to the pressure.

00:44:00.160 --> 00:44:01.280
and moved back to England.

00:44:02.310 --> 00:44:04.570
But for the remainder of the 1850s,

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:07.940
William Lloyd Garrison and Frederick Douglass

00:44:07.940 --> 00:44:09.680
would not speak to each other.

00:44:27.890 --> 00:44:29.970
Not long after the passage of the fugitive

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:36.840
of slave law, a runaway knocked on the door of a house in Brunswick, Maine. As fate would have it,

00:44:37.430 --> 00:44:43.880
he had come upon the new home of Harriet Beecher Stowe and her family, including a newborn named

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:50.440
after the baby they had buried in Cincinnati. Stowe was well aware of the punishment facing

00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:55.390
anyone who helped a runaway, but she was glad of the opportunity to defy the government.

00:44:56.830 --> 00:44:59.810
Before this law, I might have tried to send him somewhere else.

00:45:00.380 --> 00:45:07.110
She wrote her sister, as it was, all hands in the house united in making him up a bed.

00:45:10.650 --> 00:45:15.310
Like many northerners, Stowe was deeply disturbed by the fugitive slave law.

00:45:17.530 --> 00:45:20.220
For her, its cruelty was all too real.

00:45:23.780 --> 00:45:29.890
Every account of flight, of capture, tore at the agonizing memory of Charlie's death.

00:45:33.450 --> 00:45:38.410
My heart breaks at the cruelty and injustice our nation inflicts on the slave.

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:45.140
I am tormented by the thought of the slave mothers whose babes are torn from them.

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:52.550
I pray to God to let me do a little and to cause my cry for them to be heard.

00:45:54.810 --> 00:45:59.980
Leaving Cincinnati allowed Harriet to look back at those 18 years there and to...

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:08.460
process that experience of the slave riots charlie's death the fugitive slave law and it all

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:17.410
came together in a vision instead of christ on the cross she saw an image of a slave being whipped

00:46:17.410 --> 00:46:24.280
and she wrote the ending of uncle tom's cabin when uncle tom dies and when she read it to

00:46:24.280 --> 00:46:29.070
her family they were all crying and they said mama you've got to write the rest of the story

00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:39.090
On March 20th, 1852, a publisher released a book on a difficult subject by an unknown author.

00:46:41.240 --> 00:46:47.580
By the end of the week, Harriet Beecher Stowe's novel about the evils of slavery had sold 10,000 copies.

00:46:48.400 --> 00:46:50.440
Within two months, 50,000.

00:46:51.660 --> 00:46:55.740
Three paper mills feeding three printing presses running 24 hours a day.

00:46:56.330 --> 00:46:58.330
And still the publisher couldn't keep up.

00:47:01.210 --> 00:47:07.310
Soon, Uncle Tom's Cabin began reaching vast new audiences, including many who had never

00:47:07.310 --> 00:47:11.000
read a novel when it was adapted as a wildly popular play.

00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:23.160
In Uncle Tom's Cabin, Harriet Beecher Stowe made millions of Americans see slavery for

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:25.900
the first time through the eyes of its victims.

00:47:27.800 --> 00:47:29.920
The mother whose child was to be sold away.

00:47:31.180 --> 00:47:33.780
The boy watching helplessly as his sister is whipped.

00:47:34.890 --> 00:47:38.230
The runaway Eliza fleeing with her baby across the Ohio River,

00:47:38.970 --> 00:47:41.110
only one step ahead of the slave catchers.

00:47:44.180 --> 00:47:46.440
And it was said that when the novel was put on stage,

00:47:46.780 --> 00:47:51.640
there was not one dry eye when Eliza reached the other side of the river.

00:47:51.800 --> 00:47:57.750
And Harriet knew that if she could get that response from her readers,

00:47:57.950 --> 00:47:59.970
she didn't need to make arguments about slavery.

00:48:01.280 --> 00:48:03.560
She realizes that the way to do this

00:48:03.560 --> 00:48:05.490
was by playing on the heartstrings.

00:48:06.160 --> 00:48:07.860
You don't do it by playing on the outrage.

00:48:08.140 --> 00:48:09.740
You do it by convincing them

00:48:09.740 --> 00:48:11.580
that there are human beings in peril.

00:48:15.210 --> 00:48:17.370
Mrs. Stowe has walked with lighted candle

00:48:17.370 --> 00:48:19.850
through the darkest corners of the slave's soul

00:48:19.850 --> 00:48:23.300
and has unfolded the secrets

00:48:23.300 --> 00:48:25.060
of the slave's lacerated heart.

00:48:28.120 --> 00:48:29.840
That contribution to our bleeding.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:41.080
cause alone involves us in a debt of gratitude which cannot be measured. It is the most popular

00:48:41.080 --> 00:48:46.710
book and the most influential book in American history. There is no competition for that title.

00:48:47.370 --> 00:48:54.820
It's going to convert millions of Americans, not to being for immediate abolitionism, not to being

00:48:54.820 --> 00:48:59.960
for racial equality, but for being against slavery. Now that's a huge thing. That's a

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:06.520
see change. You can see Stowe taking aim at the fugitive slave law in incident after incident

00:49:06.520 --> 00:49:15.120
and basically urging her readers to defy what she thought was an anti-Christian undemocratic law.

00:49:16.370 --> 00:49:21.680
William Lloyd Garrison attended a performance of the play in New York and marveled at the

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:29.980
response of the toughs in the front rows. Oh, it was a sight worth seeing. Those ragged, coatless

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:38.110
men and boys in the pit the very material of which mobs are made cheering the strongest and sublimest

00:49:38.110 --> 00:49:48.870
anti-slavery sentiments uncle tom's cabin triggered a wave of defiance to the fugitive slave law

00:49:49.920 --> 00:49:57.940
armed mobs broke into jails to release fugitives and even murdered slave catchers and in the spring

00:49:57.940 --> 00:49:59.960
of 1854, this wave

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:06.030
of resistance came to a head when the national battle over slavery suddenly focused on a solitary

00:50:06.030 --> 00:50:15.400
prisoner in Boston's city jail. Anthony Burns had fled Virginia and slavery by stowing away on a

00:50:15.400 --> 00:50:21.400
ship bound for the north. He had avoided capture for months until a letter home revealed his

00:50:21.400 --> 00:50:28.650
whereabouts. When news of the arrest filtered out, Boston erupted in anger.

00:50:30.190 --> 00:50:33.230
While one group tried to halt the proceedings through legal maneuvers,

00:50:33.880 --> 00:50:37.280
black and white activists mounted a desperate assault on the courthouse

00:50:37.280 --> 00:50:38.920
where Burns was being held.

00:50:40.550 --> 00:50:43.350
The attack was driven back by club-wielding policemen,

00:50:43.850 --> 00:50:46.270
but not before a guard was shot and killed.

00:50:52.350 --> 00:50:55.860
When the sun rose the next morning, Burns was still in jail,

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:57.840
and the city was seething.

00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:06.030
When news of the upheaval reached Washington, President Franklin Pierce, an ardent Southern

00:51:06.030 --> 00:51:09.190
sympathizer, demonstrated the government's resolve.

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:20.140
On June 2nd, 1854, all Boston watched as hundreds of U.S. Marines, cavalry, infantry

00:51:20.140 --> 00:51:25.630
and artillery, along with the county militia and the Boston police, marched Anthony Burns

00:51:25.630 --> 00:51:28.130
from the jail to a ship waiting in the harbor.

00:51:30.760 --> 00:51:40.770
For Garrison, the work of abolition was about trying to make people come to a moment of conscience and act accordingly.

00:51:43.040 --> 00:51:47.210
But one has to see the devil in order to then turn towards God.

00:51:47.510 --> 00:51:52.170
One has to see evil in order to be committed to a life of good.

00:51:54.180 --> 00:51:57.900
Slavery marches right up into Massachusetts.

00:52:00.000 --> 00:52:02.940
And now Northerners get to see the face of evil.

00:52:05.160 --> 00:52:09.400
It seemed that the United States was committed to the preservation of slavery.

00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:12.730
Abolitionists were losing hope.

00:52:13.860 --> 00:52:16.880
They couldn't see that already they had shifted history.

00:52:18.360 --> 00:52:22.120
By refusing to disappear, to be silenced, to accommodate,

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:28.510
they had forced millions of Americans to take a stand on slavery one way or the other.

00:52:31.340 --> 00:52:35.780
The ties between North and South had been replaced by resentment and suspicion.

00:52:42.290 --> 00:52:47.180
Harrison, Douglas, Stowe, and their fellow abolitionists couldn't know it,

00:52:47.840 --> 00:52:50.860
but their long struggle had passed a tipping point.

